{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-51342",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|x-2{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ on the interval between $a=2$ and $x=2.3.$ \n\nPlugging $x=2.3$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{3!}\\,{|2.3-2|}^{3} \\\\ |{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}\\,{|0.3|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[2,2.3].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ |{f}^{(3)}(x)| & =|\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}|=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(3)}(x)|=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ is a decreasing function on the interval $[2,2.3],$ its value is greatest at $x=2.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(3)}(2)|=\\frac{2}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{6}\\,{|0.3|}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{0.3}^{3}}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8\\,000}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
